A split-out variant of mrb_vm_exec() for the pinned mruby 4.0.0 source, applied only to the wasm build this repository converts to Go.
The shared tail of the send opcodes, the block that L_SENDB_SYM labels and that OP_SEND, OP_SEND0, OP_SENDB, the OP_SSEND family, OP_SUPER, OP_SETIDX and every L_SEND_SYM fallback jump into, moves into a static function marked __attribute__((noinline)), with each case exit turned into a return code that VS_DISPATCH() decodes at the call site and each dispatcher local the body writes (irep, ci, and the value the CINFO_RESUMED path returns from mrb_vm_exec) passed by pointer.
Behavior is unchanged; what changes is the shape the Go backend sees after conversion.
mrb_vm_exec() converts into one Go method of about 29,000 lines, and CPU profiles of fib, lists and ao attribute the time to that method's own body rather than to its callees, so what the Go compiler has to register-allocate inside a single enormous function is the hot opcode bodies themselves; the send tail is the largest of them and the one every benchmark runs most.
noinline is what keeps it split: LLVM would otherwise inline this single-caller function straight back into the dispatcher. wasm-opt does not run on this build (WASM_OPT=0), so Binaryen's inliner is not a concern.
The moved code is byte-identical to the original apart from those rewrites, and it is tied to the line shape of the 4.0.0 source: a version bump needs the extraction redone against the new source, not this patch rebased onto it.
Only the send tail is split, because both wider splits measured worse.
Moving OP_ENTER and the OP_RETURN family (OP_RETURN with its OP_RETSELF/RETNIL/RETTRUE/RETFALSE entries, plus OP_BREAK and OP_RETURN_BLK, which jump into its labels) out as well made fib 17% slower than the unpatched build instead of 3% faster: a Ruby-level call then pays three out-of-line calls where it now pays one.
Keeping those inline but adding the medium-sized bodies (OP_CALL, OP_BLKCALL, OP_ARGARY, OP_GETIDX, OP_GETIDX0, OP_SETIDX, OP_APOST, OP_BLKPUSH, OP_EXEC, OP_CLASS, OP_MODULE) still cost 4.6% on fib, 4.8% on ao and 7.4% on lists against splitting the send tail alone.
Small bodies (OP_MOVE, the OP_LOADI family, the fixnum fast paths of OP_ADD/OP_SUB/OP_EQ and friends) never move: a 5-10 line dispatch state cannot carry a call.

